    "created_at": "2019-08-21T07:42:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-08-21T07:42:41Z",
    "body": "I get what you're saying and it makes sense.\r\nThe version of typescript isn't really relevant to most users throughout development.\r\n\r\nI've thought about another solution that might work and I think it solves both the irrelevancy of the specific version and the time it takes to execute `tsc --version`\r\n\r\nI develop web applications both in Javascript and TypeScript so I thought it would be nice to have something to show when I'm in a TypeScript project and that's why I started this module.\r\n\r\n![Screen Shot 2019-08-21 at 10 22 41](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/42851547/63411790-f91c4600-c3fe-11e9-8f94-053f9e891d18.png)\r\n\r\nDo you think that this indicator could be a good solution? It scans the current directory to look for files that will indicate typescript is being used in this project but it doesn't call `tsc --version` to retrieve the current typescript version. Instead it only shows that this project is with typescript.\r\nIt also makes the prompt a little bit shorter than previously.\r\n\r\nRemoving the need to call `tsc --version` also removes the need to install typescript on Docker and CI. So the testing time will be a bit shorter :)\r\n\r\nIf you want I can change the ■ to 🇹. (Like we discussed in the pull request)\r\nOr we can make it configurable but I might need some help with that because I'm a rust beginner 😅 ",
